WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.441 --> 00:00:02.705
Engineering design keeps evolving.

2
00:00:02.945 --> 00:00:04.989
Computational photonics gives us

3
00:00:04.989 --> 00:00:06.712
a useful four-stage view.

4
00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:09.916
First, classical inverse design.

5
00:00:10.277 --> 00:00:13.580
A numerical optimizer
searches for a structure

6
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:14.862
that meets a target.

7
00:00:15.122 --> 00:00:17.925
A physics solver evaluates each candidate.

8
00:00:18.907 --> 00:00:21.348
Second, machine learning assisted design.

9
00:00:21.829 --> 00:00:24.430
Neural networks learn from simulation data

10
00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:26.972
to act as fast surrogates or optimizers.

11
00:00:26.992 --> 00:00:28.232
Their predictions still need

12
00:00:28.232 --> 00:00:29.873
physics based verification.

13
00:00:30.713 --> 00:00:33.957
Third, large language
models help researchers

14
00:00:33.957 --> 00:00:36.519
write code, prepare simulations,

15
00:00:36.519 --> 00:00:37.900
and propose designs.

16
00:00:38.221 --> 00:00:39.782
The model assists the work.

17
00:00:40.063 --> 00:00:42.845
It does not replace the
numerical calculation.

18
00:00:43.907 --> 00:00:46.951
Fourth, agentic engineering
connects the steps

19
00:00:46.951 --> 00:00:48.092
into a workflow.

20
00:00:48.432 --> 00:00:50.914
The agent can plan, call tools,

21
00:00:51.235 --> 00:00:53.877
run simulations, analyze results,

22
00:00:54.078 --> 00:00:55.860
and iterate on a design.

23
00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.101
Across all four stages,

24
00:00:58.101 --> 00:00:59.722
physics remains the foundation.

25
00:00:59.922 --> 00:01:02.363
The aim is faster, more accessible design,

26
00:01:02.363 --> 00:01:04.404
while maintaining scientific validation.

27
00:01:04.604 --> 00:01:06.105
Automation changes the workflow.

28
00:01:06.305 --> 00:01:07.706
It does not remove the need

29
00:01:07.706 --> 00:01:09.166
to check the calculation.

30
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.582
Connect your AI assistant

31
00:01:13.582 --> 00:01:14.944
to your engineering tools.

32
00:01:15.545 --> 00:01:17.907
Model Context Protocol, or MCP,

33
00:01:18.648 --> 00:01:20.310
links OpenCode with tools

34
00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.632
for Gmsh and CalculiX.

35
00:01:23.793 --> 00:01:25.774
First, connect OpenCode

36
00:01:25.774 --> 00:01:27.935
to your chosen model API.

37
00:01:28.516 --> 00:01:30.237
The recording uses DeepSeek.

38
00:01:30.597 --> 00:01:33.198
That gives the assistant a model to work with.

39
00:01:33.638 --> 00:01:35.319
The tool connection comes next.

40
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.861
Then ask OpenCode to help configure

41
00:01:38.861 --> 00:01:42.123
an MCP server for CalculiX and Gmsh.

42
00:01:42.303 --> 00:01:43.504
A clear request is:

43
00:01:43.764 --> 00:01:44.904
connect these tools,

44
00:01:44.984 --> 00:01:46.345
prepare the configuration,

45
00:01:46.505 --> 00:01:48.706
and identify any missing dependencies.

46
00:01:49.807 --> 00:01:51.689
Next, test the connection.

47
00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.532
Ask whether both tools are accessible.

48
00:01:55.152 --> 00:01:56.734
The recorded example shows

49
00:01:56.734 --> 00:01:59.016
the CalculiX server as connected.

50
00:01:59.596 --> 00:02:00.857
That status is useful,

51
00:02:00.857 --> 00:02:03.320
but each intended tool still needs to be
checked.

52
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.002
State the engineering goal in plain language.

53
00:02:07.402 --> 00:02:10.864
Let the AI help coordinate the software.

54
00:02:11.385 --> 00:02:12.685
Keep connection tests

55
00:02:12.786 --> 00:02:14.026
as part of the workflow.

56
00:02:16.068 --> 00:02:18.333
A tool connection should do more than

57
00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:19.556
show a green light.

58
00:02:19.897 --> 00:02:20.839
Let's test it.

59
00:02:21.781 --> 00:02:25.206
In OpenCode, request the scientific tool
connection

60
00:02:25.647 --> 00:02:27.570
through the Model Context Protocol.

61
00:02:28.071 --> 00:02:30.575
Then ask the assistant to verify it

62
00:02:30.575 --> 00:02:32.859
with a small, concrete test.

63
00:02:33.821 --> 00:02:36.825
The recording checks the existing
configuration and

64
00:02:36.825 --> 00:02:38.166
the installed programs.

65
00:02:38.547 --> 00:02:41.470
The server was already registered, so no

66
00:02:41.471 --> 00:02:43.173
configuration change was needed.

67
00:02:44.094 --> 00:02:47.676
The recorded report describes a mesh
refinement

68
00:02:47.676 --> 00:02:49.176
test on a small bar.

69
00:02:49.797 --> 00:02:52.878
The smaller mesh size produced more
elements.

70
00:02:53.159 --> 00:02:55.900
That checks refinement behavior, not
convergence.

71
00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.242
Check the force balance and the extension

72
00:02:59.242 --> 00:02:59.802
of the bar.

73
00:03:00.362 --> 00:03:03.144
The recorded report compares both with
simple

74
00:03:03.144 --> 00:03:06.686
reference values. A connected status alone
cannot

75
00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:07.926
give you that evidence.

76
00:03:09.107 --> 00:03:10.669
Keep the limitations in view.

77
00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:13.912
The report flags end effects and
duplicated

78
00:03:13.912 --> 00:03:16.575
mesh nodes. This is an integration check,

79
00:03:16.775 --> 00:03:18.597
not an accuracy certification.

80
00:03:18.937 --> 00:03:21.199
No convergence study or bending benchmark
was

81
00:03:21.199 --> 00:03:21.560
performed.

82
00:03:22.461 --> 00:03:25.025
Connect the tools, run a small test,

83
00:03:25.226 --> 00:03:27.730
and inspect the evidence before trusting a

84
00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:28.932
larger calculation.

85
00:03:31.293 --> 00:03:32.994
Give your AI agent

86
00:03:33.134 --> 00:03:34.735
a repeatable way to work.

87
00:03:35.295 --> 00:03:37.796
MCP connects the agent to software.

88
00:03:38.157 --> 00:03:40.978
A skill file guides how it uses that software,

89
00:03:41.158 --> 00:03:43.179
with instructions, workflows, and checks.

90
00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.141
Start with the official manuals

91
00:03:46.141 --> 00:03:47.922
for CalculiX and Gmsh.

92
00:03:48.202 --> 00:03:49.322
Ask a capable model

93
00:03:49.322 --> 00:03:51.143
to draft a SKILL.md file

94
00:03:51.143 --> 00:03:52.404
with clear instructions,

95
00:03:52.724 --> 00:03:53.944
a recommended workflow,

96
00:03:53.944 --> 00:03:55.145
and validation steps.

97
00:03:55.585 --> 00:03:57.666
Then review the draft against those manuals

98
00:03:57.666 --> 00:03:58.866
and benchmark examples.

99
00:03:59.947 --> 00:04:01.408
Place the reviewed file

100
00:04:01.509 --> 00:04:02.951
in its own skill folder

101
00:04:02.991 --> 00:04:05.775
inside your project’s OpenCode skills
directory.

102
00:04:06.036 --> 00:04:07.598
Include a name and description

103
00:04:07.598 --> 00:04:08.900
in the file’s frontmatter.

104
00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:10.622
Then check that OpenCode

105
00:04:10.622 --> 00:04:12.164
discovers and loads the skill.

106
00:04:13.406 --> 00:04:14.628
Build your library

107
00:04:14.628 --> 00:04:16.289
one verified skill at a time.

108
00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:18.652
Add your engineering experience

109
00:04:18.712 --> 00:04:20.654
as you improve each workflow.

110
00:04:21.355 --> 00:04:23.337
Reusable instructions make the assistant

111
00:04:23.337 --> 00:04:24.938
more useful for your domain.

112
00:04:27.101 --> 00:04:28.723
Turn a generated skill

113
00:04:28.924 --> 00:04:31.107
into a checked engineering workflow.

114
00:04:31.388 --> 00:04:33.311
This recording follows the handoff

115
00:04:33.511 --> 00:04:35.615
from ChatGPT to OpenCode

116
00:04:35.615 --> 00:04:38.099
for CalculiX and Gmsh.

117
00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.881
Start with a precise request in ChatGPT.

118
00:04:42.542 --> 00:04:45.823
Ask for a SKILL.md for CalculiX and Gmsh,

119
00:04:46.103 --> 00:04:48.684
with guidance checked against official
manuals,

120
00:04:49.004 --> 00:04:51.805
verified commands, and corrected
inconsistencies.

121
00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:54.386
That request sets the scope for the package.

122
00:04:55.347 --> 00:04:58.508
The recorded response offers a SKILL.md,

123
00:04:59.189 --> 00:05:01.890
technical references, runnable examples,

124
00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:03.310
and a verification script.

125
00:05:03.851 --> 00:05:05.691
Review the package and its workflows

126
00:05:05.691 --> 00:05:06.852
before installing it.

127
00:05:07.512 --> 00:05:09.893
A generated response is a starting point

128
00:05:09.893 --> 00:05:10.693
for checking.

129
00:05:11.773 --> 00:05:14.874
Next, bring the downloaded skill into
OpenCode.

130
00:05:15.135 --> 00:05:17.175
The recorded request asks the agent

131
00:05:17.175 --> 00:05:19.116
to place it in the skills directory,

132
00:05:19.396 --> 00:05:22.478
verify installation, and configure future use.

133
00:05:23.058 --> 00:05:26.159
Keep one clear skill folder with its SKILL.md.

134
00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.381
Then read the verification report.

135
00:05:29.861 --> 00:05:32.662
OpenCode reports that the skill is
discoverable,

136
00:05:33.002 --> 00:05:35.843
visible in the session, and reloads
successfully.

137
00:05:36.164 --> 00:05:39.185
It also reports a Gmsh mesh smoke test

138
00:05:39.465 --> 00:05:41.066
and an input-file export.

139
00:05:41.386 --> 00:05:42.766
These are specific checks.

140
00:05:43.867 --> 00:05:45.229
The warnings matter too.

141
00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:50.174
The recorded report lists CalculiX version

142
00:05:50.174 --> 00:05:51.115
two point eleven

143
00:05:51.655 --> 00:05:54.458
against a two point twenty-three skill
baseline.

144
00:05:54.959 --> 00:05:57.281
It also flags missing NumPy

145
00:05:57.361 --> 00:05:59.223
and Gmsh Python modules,

146
00:05:59.463 --> 00:06:02.106
and says no solver was executed.

147
00:06:03.388 --> 00:06:05.811
Make the report your next-step checklist.

148
00:06:06.011 --> 00:06:07.594
Resolve the reported gaps

149
00:06:07.714 --> 00:06:10.177
and verify the workflow in your environment.

150
00:06:10.618 --> 00:06:13.362
ChatGPT helps prepare the instructions.

151
00:06:13.703 --> 00:06:15.766
OpenCode helps put them to work.

152
00:06:17.888 --> 00:06:20.975
Before running a simulation, build a plan

153
00:06:21.136 --> 00:06:22.318
you can verify.

154
00:06:23.321 --> 00:06:25.665
The task is a steel bar stretched

155
00:06:25.665 --> 00:06:28.068
by zero point zero one millimeters.

156
00:06:28.629 --> 00:06:30.953
Ask an AI planner to organize the

157
00:06:30.953 --> 00:06:34.078
work, including the tools, the stress
calculation,

158
00:06:34.178 --> 00:06:34.879
and the checks.

159
00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:36.921
Use five phases.

160
00:06:37.701 --> 00:06:39.362
Verify the tool connections.

161
00:06:39.382 --> 00:06:42.983
Prepare the geometry, material, and
constraints.

162
00:06:43.443 --> 00:06:45.704
Generate the mesh and run the solver.

163
00:06:46.344 --> 00:06:48.785
Validate the solution against theory and
mesh

164
00:06:48.785 --> 00:06:52.327
refinement. Then visualize stress and
displacement.

165
00:06:53.547 --> 00:06:55.949
Extension alone does not determine stress.

166
00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:58.592
You also need the bar's length and

167
00:06:58.592 --> 00:07:01.374
material properties. For a uniform bar in

168
00:07:01.374 --> 00:07:04.717
small, linear elastic tension, stress
equals Young's

169
00:07:04.717 --> 00:07:07.200
modulus times extension divided by length.

170
00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:10.642
Pass the structured plan to your execution

171
00:07:10.642 --> 00:07:13.443
assistant, such as OpenCode with DeepSeek.

172
00:07:14.003 --> 00:07:16.944
Keep the model assumptions, units, output
files,

173
00:07:16.944 --> 00:07:20.166
and validation criteria explicit, so each
phase

174
00:07:20.166 --> 00:07:21.647
can be checked and repeated.

175
00:07:22.828 --> 00:07:23.671
Plan the work.

176
00:07:24.173 --> 00:07:25.095
Check the physics.

177
00:07:25.417 --> 00:07:27.403
Then show what the evidence supports.

178
00:07:29.808 --> 00:07:31.171
OpenCode has finished.

179
00:07:31.252 --> 00:07:32.896
Let's inspect the evidence

180
00:07:32.896 --> 00:07:34.379
behind the calculation.

181
00:07:35.301 --> 00:07:37.243
After submitting the prepared prompt,

182
00:07:37.584 --> 00:07:40.428
OpenCode organizes the
run into five stages:

183
00:07:40.648 --> 00:07:43.052
connection, model setup, simulation,

184
00:07:43.052 --> 00:07:44.574
validation, and visualization.

185
00:07:44.895 --> 00:07:46.497
Use that structure to inspect

186
00:07:46.497 --> 00:07:48.019
the evidence behind the answer.

187
00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.521
This example is a ten millimeter steel bar

188
00:07:51.521 --> 00:07:53.883
with a one millimeter
square cross section.

189
00:07:54.323 --> 00:07:55.764
The prescribed extension is

190
00:07:55.764 --> 00:07:57.724
zero point zero one millimeters.

191
00:07:58.145 --> 00:08:00.306
The report lists the material properties,

192
00:08:00.306 --> 00:08:01.726
mesh, and boundary conditions.

193
00:08:02.408 --> 00:08:04.550
There are two complementary runs.

194
00:08:04.831 --> 00:08:06.454
The direct CalculiX benchmark

195
00:08:06.454 --> 00:08:07.755
prescribes the extension.

196
00:08:08.216 --> 00:08:10.239
The MCP cross-check applies

197
00:08:10.239 --> 00:08:11.721
an equivalent axial force.

198
00:08:12.082 --> 00:08:13.805
Keep those loading methods separate

199
00:08:13.805 --> 00:08:15.126
when you compare the results.

200
00:08:15.747 --> 00:08:18.429
The direct test reports consistent results

201
00:08:18.429 --> 00:08:19.990
across three meshes.

202
00:08:20.351 --> 00:08:22.332
For the force-controlled cross-check,

203
00:08:22.453 --> 00:08:23.954
compare midspan stress

204
00:08:24.014 --> 00:08:26.275
and average end displacement with theory.

205
00:08:26.816 --> 00:08:29.338
The report warns that raw maxima include

206
00:08:29.338 --> 00:08:31.520
constraint and load-distribution effects.

207
00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:34.222
The validation dashboard brings

208
00:08:34.222 --> 00:08:35.403
those checks together:

209
00:08:35.743 --> 00:08:38.286
axial displacement, lateral contraction,

210
00:08:38.686 --> 00:08:40.928
stress, and comparison with theory.

211
00:08:41.009 --> 00:08:43.411
Keep the plot labels and
the loading assumptions

212
00:08:43.411 --> 00:08:45.293
visible as you review the result.

213
00:08:46.174 --> 00:08:48.957
Next, open the simulation folder in Linux.

214
00:08:49.558 --> 00:08:53.021
It contains the input
deck, mesh, solver logs,

215
00:08:53.141 --> 00:08:54.703
extracted results, and plots.

216
00:08:55.183 --> 00:08:57.366
These files let you trace the calculation

217
00:08:57.366 --> 00:08:58.467
and inspect the setup.

218
00:08:59.447 --> 00:09:01.088
Open the input file to review

219
00:09:01.088 --> 00:09:03.029
the material and boundary conditions,

220
00:09:03.269 --> 00:09:05.730
then check the solver log for warnings.

221
00:09:06.191 --> 00:09:08.132
This example is a small strain,

222
00:09:08.132 --> 00:09:09.432
linear elastic check.

223
00:09:09.632 --> 00:09:11.173
Good automation leaves evidence

224
00:09:11.173 --> 00:09:11.873
you can review.
